WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.920
Hey, this is Presh Talwalkar. Can you solve for x in the following equation?

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:12.580
This problem was posed by Srinivasa Ramanujan in the Journal of the Indian mathematical society over a hundred years ago

00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:18.000
Give this problem a try and when you're ready keep watching the video for the solution

00:00:21.010 --> 00:00:26.039
You might try and find an approximation in a calculator a spreadsheet or computer program

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:29.580
You'll get that x is equal to about three

00:00:30.609 --> 00:00:33.359
In fact, x is equal to exactly three

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:36.090
Let's see why

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:44.520
Three is equal to the square root of nine. Nine equals one plus eight and eight factors out into two times four

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:48.389
Four can be expressed as the square root of sixteen

00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:56.849
We now have a square root inside of another square root. We've come to the first iteration of this nested radical

00:00:58.210 --> 00:01:00.210
Can we get to the next stage?

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:06.809
Well 16 equals one plus fifteen and fifteen factors out into three times five

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:13.979
Five can be re-written as the square root of twenty-five, and we've come to the next iteration of the nested radical

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:18.390
25 equals 1 plus 24 and

00:01:19.090 --> 00:01:21.390
24 factors out into four times six

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:25.860
Six is the square root of 36, and we've come to the next stage

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:29.339
In fact I claim this pattern continues indefinitely

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:36.029
Therefore we proved this infinite nested radical is equal to the original term which is three.

00:01:37.119 --> 00:01:39.119
But how do we know the pattern continues?

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:44.939
If you take a look at the final square root, we always have the square root of a perfect square

00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:49.649
We want to know can we get from this term to the next term?

00:01:49.990 --> 00:01:53.699
That is if we have the final square root being a perfect square

00:01:53.920 --> 00:02:00.659
where the coefficient is being multiplied by is 2 less than the perfect square can we always take that perfect square and

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:04.889
increment the perfect square and increment the term that is being multiplied by

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:08.460
This is true

00:02:09.399 --> 00:02:13.169
Algebraically we start out with our original term and we expand out the square

00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:18.539
We can then rewrite this as for equaling one plus three

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:26.480
We now factor out n squared plus 4n plus 3 into the terms n plus 1 pimes n plus 3

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:31.500
Now we write a radical where we have the square root of N plus 3 squared.

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:33.540
And this gets us to what we wanted to prove

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:38.320
So what any iteration we can always get to the next stage and that shows this pattern will continue

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:41.220
Did you figure it out?

